MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Closes the Phase 3 EPCS READ pre-amble hang that survived patches
0008-0014 (every wrapper-only fix refuted across 8 distinct
bitstreams).  HW-verified 2026-05-17 night: FAST_READ returns real
ALINX EPCS bytes (0x7d 0x21 0x0f 0x00 @ addr 0), wait_dv terminates
at i=0 on every byte — the megafunction-to-asmiblock-to-flash path
is sound when do_fast_read drives the read FSM.

CHANGES

1) rtl/ip/epcs_ctrl.v — qmegawiz silent regen.  Edited Retrieval-info
   block: PORT_FAST_READ STRING "PORT_USED" (was UNUSED), added
   USED_PORT + CONNECT lines for `fast_read`.  Then `cd rtl/ip &&
   qmegawiz -silent epcs_ctrl.v` regenerated the body.  New module
   exposes `input fast_read;` (line 74), latches it into
   `fast_read_reg` on (~busy & rden) (line 829-837), drives
   `do_fast_read = ... & fast_read_wire` (line 1737), and brings the
   stage3 do_wait_dummyclk FSM-unlock at line 383 OR-term live:
     | ((stage3_wire & do_fast_read) & do_wait_dummyclk)
   This unlock is fast_read-exclusive and is the actual escape from
   the pre-amble gate that plain READ lacks.

   Bonus effect: with do_fast_read live, end_read_reg's data_in OR
   path `((~rden) & (do_read | do_fast_read) & data_valid & end_rbyte)`
   has a non-zero consumer chain regardless of `read` port state.
   Combined with patch 0012's read_address connection, the end_read_reg
   register and its fanout chain (read_add_cntr, add_rollover_reg) are
   now fully preserved through synthesis.  map.rpt "Registers Removed"
   shows only `read_add_cntr|counter_reg_bit[24]` (unused 25-bit-
   counter upper bit — address space is 24-bit, normal prune).

   EMPIRICAL SURPRISE — read/fast_read mutex.  qmegawiz 21.1 Lite
   silently STRIPS the `read` USED_PORT entry from Retrieval info and
   drops the `read` input from the generated module when
   port_fast_read=PORT_USED.  Re-adding `read` USED_PORT and re-running
   qmegawiz silent does not help — stripped again every regen.  Read
   and FAST_READ are mutually exclusive in this toolchain; pick one.
   See memory feedback-quartus-clearbox-megafunctions §"Port mutex
   gotcha".  Adopted FAST_READ as the canonical read path.

2) rtl/wb_altasmi_parallel.v — wrapper edits to follow the megafunction's
   new port set.
     - Add CTRL.b8=fast_read level bit: ctrl_fast_read reg + xbus_dat_w[8]
       latch + reset.  Wire .fast_read(ctrl_fast_read) on epcs_ctrl
       instance.
     - Remove .read(ctrl_read) on epcs_ctrl instance and delete
       ctrl_read reg entirely — the megafunction no longer has a `read`
       input.  CTRL.b0 is now reserved; doc comment updated.
     - Readback at 0x00 returns {fast_read, ..., rd_status, sec_erase,
       write, 1'b0} (b0=0 as reserved).

3) sw/stage2_loader/epcs.c — driver changes.
     - Add CTRL_RDEN_DROP (1<<7) and CTRL_FAST_READ (1<<8) macros.
       Remove CTRL_READ (now reserved bit).
     - Delete epcs_read() entirely (the megafunction no longer
       implements the plain READ path; calling it via CTRL.b0 would
       spin forever since b0 is dropped by the wrapper).
     - Add epcs_fast_read(addr, len, buf):
         *WB_AM_ADDR = addr & 0xFFFFFF;
         *WB_AM_CTRL = CTRL_FAST_READ;
         for each byte: pulse SHIFT_BYTES, wait DV, read DATAOUT
         *WB_AM_CTRL = CTRL_FAST_READ | CTRL_RDEN_DROP;  // end_read_reg fires
         wait_not_busy();
         *WB_AM_CTRL = 0;
       Critical: NO wait_not_busy() between CTRL=FAST_READ and the
       shift loop — busy_wire stays asserted for the entire operation
       (do_fast_read=1 → busy_wire=1) and would always hang.  Pre-amble
       (opcode 0x0B + 24-bit addr + 1 dummy byte) runs inside the first
       SHIFT_BYTES iteration's wait_data_valid window.

4) sw/stage2_loader/epcs.h — declare epcs_fast_read, remove epcs_read.

5) sw/stage2_loader/main.c — mode 'P' diagnostic Phase C.
     - Add DC_FAST_READ (1<<8) local macro.
     - Phase B docstring updated: now a CONTROL EXPERIMENT (CTRL.b0
       writes silently dropped by wrapper; expected B1 STATUS=0x00,
       B2 OK at i=0, B4 TIMEOUT — confirms b0 dead).
     - Phase C — FAST_READ 4B @0 with RDEN_DROP termination.  Reads
       4 bytes via SHIFT_BYTES pulses, bounded wait_dv per byte,
       prints DATAOUT.  Terminates via CTRL=FAST_READ|RDEN_DROP.
       Discriminates NH9 outcomes: real bytes (success), all 0xff
       (data-path issue), TIMEOUT (Plan B needed).

6) .gitignore — hide qmegawiz silent regen scratch (rtl/ip/greybox_tmp/),
   .pof output, .asm disassembly, raw_exe text formats, orchestrator
   apply-marker.  Keeps `git status` clean during HW test cycles.

VERIFIED ON SILICON (capture md5 9d80d60c, 2487 B at
docs/captures/diag_nh9_fast_read_ok.log in the orchestrator repo):
  C1 after CTRL=FAST_READ STATUS=0x03      ← busy + DV asserted
  C2 byte=0 wait_dv i=0 DATAOUT=0x7d        ← real ALINX byte 0
  C2 byte=1 wait_dv i=0 DATAOUT=0x21
  C2 byte=2 wait_dv i=0 DATAOUT=0x0f
  C2 byte=3 wait_dv i=0 DATAOUT=0x00

KNOWN SIDE EFFECT (regression): Phase A RDSR now hangs (busy stuck
at STATUS=0x01).  Prior bitstreams: A2 done i=0x6 OK.  NH9:
A2 TIMEOUT at i=10M.  Likely the `read` port removal also altered
the read_status FSM completion path.  Impact: epcs_read_status()
broken → poll_wip_clear() broken → write/erase phases blocked
until fixed.  Not blocking READ closure.  Follow-up audit needed
on epcs_ctrl.v end_op_wire (line ~1736) read_status branches.

BUDGET (post-NH9, post-P1):
  LE     8880 / 10320 (86%, +58 from NH8 v3's 8822)
  ELF    16280 B (+736 from NH8 v3's 15544 B)
  Slack  -1.003 ns setup CLOCK (was -0.968 — small regression from
         denser routing, design has worked at negative slack before)
  RBF    md5 5145a6d7c5362390d2086acb64dec627 (368011 B)

REFUTED HYPOTHESES (don't re-test): NH1, NH3, NH4, NH5 (mostly),
NH6, NH7 (partial — preserved counter), NH8 v1/v2/v3 (rden polarity).
